  "preamble": "The labial-velar glide [w] is fortified to the voiced bilabial fricative [β], losing its velar component and gaining frication. In Koine Greek the offglide of the diphthongs αυ and ευ, still [w] in 1st-century CE Egyptian Koine, became a consonant by the 4th century CE: [β] before voiced sounds and vowels, [ɸ] before voiceless ones. Medieval Greek then shifted [β] to [v].",
  "phoneticEffects": "Fortition, Spirantization",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Koine Greek",
      "languageFamily": "Hellenic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "1st-century CE Egyptian Koine αὐλή /awˈle̝/ 'courtyard' (Attic /au̯.lɛ̌ː/)",
          "to": "4th-century CE Koine /aˈβli/, Medieval /aˈvli/"
        },
        {
          "from": "1st-century CE Egyptian Koine ταῦρος /ˈtaw.ros/ 'bull' (Attic /tâu̯.ros/)",
          "to": "4th-century CE Koine /ˈta.βros/, Medieval /ˈta.vros/"
        },
        {
          "from": "1st-century CE Egyptian Koine αὔριον /ˈaw.ri.on/ 'tomorrow' (Attic /ǎu̯.ri.on/)",
          "to": "4th-century CE Koine /ˈa.βri.on/, Medieval /ˈa.vri.on/",
          "note": "Before voiceless consonants the same glide became [ɸ]: αὐτός /aʍˈtos/ > Koine /aɸˈtos/. Forms are Wiktionary's period pronunciations of Greek."
        }
      ]
